T(32, "32-6", "Letters Cast in Metal", "Recount (Historical)", [
"Before the middle of the fifteenth century, every book in Europe had to be copied out by hand. Scribes, most of them monks, sat for months at a time bent over a single volume, dipping their pens and forming each letter with painstaking care. The results could be beautiful, but they were so slow to produce and so costly to buy that only churches, universities and wealthy families could afford more than a handful of books. For almost everybody else, reading material was scarce.",
"In the German city of Mainz, around 1450, a goldsmith named Johannes Gutenberg began to wonder whether there might be a quicker way. Printers of the time already carved whole pages into blocks of wood, but a block could print only the page it had been cut for, and the carving itself was laborious. Gutenberg’s insight was to make each letter separately, so that the same pieces could be arranged to form any page and then rearranged for the next.",
"Making such letters demanded all of his skill as a metalworker. He cut the shape of each character into a hard metal punch, pressed the punch into a softer piece of metal to form a mould, and poured a hot mixture of lead, tin and antimony into the mould, where it cooled into a small block bearing a raised, back-to-front letter. Hundreds of identical letters could be cast from a single mould, and because they were made of metal they were tough enough to be used again and again.",
"Gutenberg also had to find an ink that would cling to metal, since the watery ink used by scribes simply ran off. After many experiments he produced a thick, sticky ink made with oil. For the press itself he adapted a heavy wooden screw press of the kind used to squeeze juice from grapes. The letters were arranged in a frame, line by line, and inked; a sheet of paper was laid on top, and the screw was turned to press the paper down firmly. When the sheet was lifted away, the page was printed, and the same letters could be inked again at once.",
"Gutenberg’s greatest project was a Latin Bible, which occupied him and his workers for several years. Roughly a hundred and eighty copies were printed, each running to well over a thousand pages, and a scribe would have needed a very long time to copy even one. The work was expensive, however, and Gutenberg had borrowed heavily to pay for it. When the debt fell due, he lost his workshop, and his former partners carried on printing without him.",
"The idea, though, could not be stopped. Printers set up presses in one city after another, and by about 1500 there were presses in more than two hundred European towns. In 1476 William Caxton opened the first press in England, at Westminster, and began to print books in English. As books grew cheaper, more people learned to read, and news, ideas and knowledge travelled faster than they ever had before.",
], [
 dict(d="2b",k="short",m=1,q="Who copied books before printing presses were used, according to paragraph 1?",a="Scribes, most of them monks.",g="Accept: scribes / monks."),
 dict(d="2a",k="mc",m=1,q="In paragraph 2, what does the word “laborious” mean?",opts=["Very quick and easy","Slow and needing a lot of effort","Beautiful to look at","Dangerous"],a="B"),
 dict(d="2b",k="find",m=1,q="Find and copy the word from paragraph 2 that tells you Gutenberg’s job.",a="goldsmith",g="Accept “goldsmith” only."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="Why could a carved wooden block not be used to print any page, according to paragraph 2?",a="It could print only the one page it had been carved for.",g="Accept any answer saying a block could only print its own page / could not be rearranged."),
 dict(d="2c",k="order",m=1,q="Number the steps from 1 to 4 to show how a letter was made in Gutenberg’s workshop.",items=["Hot metal was poured into the mould.","A punch was pressed into softer metal.","The shape of the character was cut into a punch.","The cooled block was ready to be used to print."],a=[3,2,1,4],g=Q2),
 dict(d="2b",k="tf",m=1,q="Tick True or False for each sentence.",items=["Gutenberg’s ink was thick and made with oil.","His press was adapted from one used for squeezing juice from grapes.","Only one copy of the Latin Bible was printed.","Caxton opened the first press in England in 1476."],a=["T","T","F","T"],g=TF),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=2,q="Give two reasons why metal letters were a better idea than carving a whole page of wood. Use paragraphs 2 and 3.",a="The letters could be rearranged to make any page; hundreds of identical letters could be cast from one mould; metal letters were tough enough to be used again and again.",g="1 mark each for two different reasons. Max 2."),
 dict(d="2d",k="mc",m=1,q="Why did Gutenberg need a new kind of ink?",opts=["The ink used by scribes was too expensive.","The ink used by scribes was too thick to write with.","The ink used by scribes would not cling to metal.","The ink used by scribes was the wrong colour."],a="C"),
 dict(d="2g",k="short",m=1,q="The writer says the idea “could not be stopped”. What does this phrase suggest about printing?",a="That it spread quickly and everybody wanted it.",g="Accept any answer about it spreading fast, being unstoppable or too useful to hold back."),
 dict(d="2a",k="short",m=1,q="In paragraph 5, what does the word “borrowed” tell you about how Gutenberg paid for the Bible?",a="He used money that belonged to somebody else and had to pay it back.",g="Accept: he took a loan / owed money to others."),
 dict(d="2d",k="short",m=1,q="What does the ending of paragraph 5 suggest about the debt?",a="That Gutenberg could not repay it, so he lost his workshop.",g="Accept any answer saying he could not pay it back or had to give up his workshop."),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which of these is the best heading for paragraph 6?",opts=["The end of Gutenberg’s workshop","How ink was made","Printing spreads across Europe","Life as a monk"],a="C"),
 dict(d="2c",k="mc",m=1,q="Which sentence best sums up the whole text?",opts=["Gutenberg’s system of movable metal letters made books quicker and cheaper to produce, and printing spread widely.","Scribes were more skilful than printers.","Gutenberg became rich from his Bible.","Printing began in England in 1476."],a="A"),
 dict(d="2e",k="short",m=1,q="Use the last paragraph to say what you think happened to the number of people who could read as books became cheaper.",a="It grew, because more people could afford books and learned to read.",g="Accept any answer saying more people learned to read."),
])
